# control-panel.yml -- the operator control panel on its own host, behind a TLS
# CLIENT CERTIFICATE gate at the edge. RENDERED by gremion-render into
# etc/edge/control-panel.yml under the Gremion root, after the switch that has
# written the `panel` service (gremion-deploy step 10, or gremion-switch itself on a host
# built by hand; `gremion-render --control-panel-only` is the same step by hand); never edited by hand and never placed by hand.
# Runbook: docs/runbooks/control-panel-access.md.
#
# It exists on a host ONLY when the install opts in, and gremion-render decides
# between three outcomes, always deleting a stale file first:
#   - CONTROL_PANEL_ORIGIN is empty or absent in state.env: the install has not
#     opted in. The file is deleted and the render SUCCEEDS (exit 0): no router, the
#     panel stays behind `gremion-tenant panel`, exactly as before.
#   - CONTROL_PANEL_ORIGIN is exactly https://<the control host> (CONTROL_PANEL_HOST
#     in state.env, else control.<PLATFORM_DOMAIN>) and the
#     opt-in is complete (the operator CA certificate at etc/edge/mtls/operator-ca.pem,
#     the same origin and docker-compose.control-public.yml in both app envs, a
#     certificate resolver): this file is written.
#   - the key is set but anything of that is missing or wrong, or the key is
#     off while an app env still carries the opt-in: the file is deleted and the
#     render REFUSES (exit 2). A half-configured opt-in never leaves a router.
#
# WHY A FILE AND NOT LABELS. A TLS option cannot be declared by a Traefik docker
# label, so it needs the file provider. The router sits in this file for the same
# reason: a router can name `control-panel-mtls` only from the provider that
# declares it. active.yml (rewritten by gremion-switch) carries only the
# colour-bound service `panel`, and NO middleware may live there; this file
# declares none either. The three it chains are declared on the Traefik
# container's labels (docker-compose.prod.yml), the only container guaranteed to
# exist whenever a router does.
#
# What each part buys (all MEASURED against the pinned Traefik by
# test/host/integration/control-panel-mtls-proof.sh, not read from the docs):
#   - clientAuthType RequireAndVerifyClientCert: a connection without a
#     certificate, or with one another CA signed, never gets an HTTP response;
#   - the option is per host (SNI): every other host on websecure keeps the
#     default option and asks nobody for a certificate. A request whose SNI is
#     another host but whose Host header is this one is answered 421, never
#     routed;
#   - a CA file that is missing or not PEM does not open the host: Traefik
#     closes every connection to it (a "special closing route") and logs an
#     error. It does NOT re-read the file when only the file changes; a changed
#     CA takes effect when this file (or any file in this directory) is
#     rewritten, which `gremion-operator-ca install` does;
#   - the rule leaves out /healthz and /api/internal/: the first answers anyone,
#     the second is the colour switch's evict endpoint. Neither belongs on the
#     internet; Traefik answers 404 for both (and for every spelling it
#     normalises to them). The panel's own gate still authenticates everything
#     else;
#   - the chain is the host pin FIRST (gremion-verify holds every router to
#     it), then HSTS, then edge-ratelimit-portal (100/min, burst 50, per
#     connection address). The tier is chosen for what this host SERVES: a page
#     of the panel is the document plus about a dozen scripts, styles and data
#     requests, sent at once from one address, and the auth tier (30/min, burst
#     10) would answer the tail of every first load with 429 (measured by
#     test/host/integration/control-panel-mtls-proof.sh, check 10). The password
#     and the second factor are checked by Keycloak on the PLATFORM host, whose
#     ops-realm login path carries the auth tier (router keycloak-ops in
#     docker-compose.prod.yml); this host sends the browser there and takes the
#     code back, behind the client-certificate gate;
#   - HSTS stays edge-hsts-platform, which sends includeSubDomains. A browser
#     applies that to the host that sent it and the names BELOW it, never to its
#     parent or its siblings: on the default host control.<PLATFORM_DOMAIN> that is
#     nothing the platform does not own, and on a CONTROL_PANEL_HOST outside the
#     platform domain (panel.example.com) it covers panel.example.com and its own
#     sub-names only, not example.com and not any customer host.
#
# Tokens (the two in the router below, in dollar-brace form): CONTROL_HOST, the
# control host gremion-render resolved (CONTROL_PANEL_HOST in state.env, else
# control.<PLATFORM_DOMAIN>; it may lie outside the platform domain), and
# TRAEFIK_CERT_RESOLVER. Nothing else is substituted. gremion-verify holds the
# rendered file to exactly this shape (check_control_panel_file).
tls:
  options:
    control-panel-mtls:
      minVersion: VersionTLS12
      clientAuth:
        caFiles:
          - /etc/traefik/dynamic/mtls/operator-ca.pem
        clientAuthType: RequireAndVerifyClientCert
http:
  routers:
    control-panel:
      entryPoints: ["websecure"]
      rule: "Host(`${CONTROL_HOST}`) && !PathPrefix(`/healthz`) && !PathPrefix(`/api/internal`)"
      service: "panel@file"
      middlewares:
        - "edge-hostpin-control@docker"
        - "edge-hsts-platform@docker"
        - "edge-ratelimit-portal@docker"
      tls:
        certResolver: "${TRAEFIK_CERT_RESOLVER}"
        options: "control-panel-mtls"
